result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/2acddf617eda8211e45d281329051f08a8b83788e24b70ebe8d526b58b195bba.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,3)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=5+3+3+1=12.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-122965/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122965"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "6f55e259-c8f7-5dd9-ab39-f4ec97b8cca3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "6c2463e0-97e4-5305-9a4a-0194e4387f27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "8b06600e-fc47-5dbc-ad3c-2d55e6fb02f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" :answer/id #uuid "02f3012a-4bdf-5e92-8b31-dd4f161f1178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "ce78f1a2-b4a8-53aa-8ea7-8c44a567d711" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5e7c3c-8550-5f46-a0a7-338080b27271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75255/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-75255/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-75255/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-75255/selection/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" "q-75255/selection/a38ef12f135c1e6010d68ed21f81bc27372a73a756455b7066f6182c46018cc8" "q-75255/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75255" :question/id #uuid "3f272e6a-b67d-5c18-b295-5bef72a286d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75255" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/e70e2cf07b27a91c34e59435229302f3b2127b15a2f39049c0a8389cd6f59863.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/aa3858dba149ebf5650ed06f75444244494fe0a961f897100914d54f6d7c0b49.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=8 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=8+5+3+0=16.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75255/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75255"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-75035"] :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer/id #uuid "677db18a-fcac-54d1-8857-3a467d432ded" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "82ef1c3e-09cb-5ad0-bfc7-889b54cb52f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040/selection/56c57502ed9362f3f416b4398e79545a0252f6594946b2d60ff940ea8bef1e7b" :answer/id #uuid "68ab3f74-7346-59c0-9a4d-61798056d306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040/selection/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" :answer/id #uuid "0e71cfc7-c6cf-5882-bd31-ccdeabb5b166" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040/selection/85f23a53be0e95a7e191561cc5a77501369717526aae5254cc8d13ed8080d997" :answer/id #uuid "f88da270-fb30-5a5f-af31-5813fa7088fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f914be0b-4a43-5aea-99fa-b140ab7e6dfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75040/selection/56c57502ed9362f3f416b4398e79545a0252f6594946b2d60ff940ea8bef1e7b" :answer-field/choices ["q-75040/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" "q-75040/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" "q-75040/selection/56c57502ed9362f3f416b4398e79545a0252f6594946b2d60ff940ea8bef1e7b" "q-75040/selection/12b48b00f9c1ff14570ca66bc629787a88856efba1cda24cfb28da58c148801a" "q-75040/selection/85f23a53be0e95a7e191561cc5a77501369717526aae5254cc8d13ed8080d997"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75040" :question/id #uuid "b45ef0a9-5ca0-51f8-b366-e36372f17811" :question/math-academy-id "q-75040" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+10.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+10$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/d7e548ae50bc8c7f0e33e8bb43ba8950788f735af103c4923d50d147995a3bb6.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/c6bdb6a479878c3fed94923a5db19d5e55db3431b685344d54b9a384f70a60e3.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=10 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,0)=11 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=11 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =10+11+11+12 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75040/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75040"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "e265f0bd-ff91-57f9-baac-4d6322cc9b33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer/id #uuid "4025005c-099c-5b03-bd85-c8e15b004e05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "47f9686f-fa22-5a07-94f1-ae7c9af40d4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "ab473f11-4cbd-5c1c-972b-57da88c085ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "c854148a-cdcd-5ea8-ad3b-1ba7089153e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "601deace-2b17-5d75-b0b2-1763d24d6366" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-122983/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer-field/choices ["q-122983/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-122983/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" "q-122983/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-122983/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-122983/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-122983" :question/id #uuid "d2dbee57-6abe-5f32-af00-c75c7d933c80" :question/math-academy-id "q-122983" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/7310b1302c35084aaef5a8b2316f59c0fbd806bc9314a51fe77167988d01f343.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/ce58eb1c2ee59cf97aa77fb9e840578e7a99f094b11b2e282317aad69ae31b7a.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/cec70986dea862392342377d83adbfa534ce0f3aa87cf2161c2f88eb83fbed58.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(0,2)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-122983/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-122983"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230/selection/abe476e447976564bc5c670fe73789666287b967d49bbed12b1d85144449e63e" :answer/id #uuid "27b62375-2fe8-52ac-a3bd-d9e0177ae998" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "2957a788-3e6b-5297-bc65-d24b39c46db7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" :answer/id #uuid "ee700184-217c-52e7-8947-7d339944fde3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer/id #uuid "9016dcf1-237d-5dc8-b22d-41637ce20225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "04e94270-3f31-5041-b7b2-8f4f9de9c7f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0ede59-6fc5-561a-a8db-bd5cc2df3829" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75230/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer-field/choices ["q-75230/selection/abe476e447976564bc5c670fe73789666287b967d49bbed12b1d85144449e63e" "q-75230/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-75230/selection/a84d639d380e10cb74c92005685f9f65779377dfb51342c280c63c645679bbea" "q-75230/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" "q-75230/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75230" :question/id #uuid "c690e767-ce40-5114-836c-aee40e57608f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75230" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,4]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3,4},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9b26ea696afda59a7181f062c7c9fab4be373035678eafa0da51bf2b22f9f4b3.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})+{m}_{23}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $6$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}+{m}_{13}+{m}_{23}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/70e4f9cd213e0dc4429e21cacf5c375d4def1e9ba021f05e45ee6e7820c5ff36.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8135011b8384a668d4bab28282220bb0560e1ae64c2f1d623822435349067f18.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(1,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{23} & =f(2,3)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2+3+3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-75230/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75230"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "720e5910-6acd-5e3c-ae73-73c1dd1e07ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "791fad60-3a39-52a2-88eb-7b72c986a22b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "fe2372d2-8d7d-51c5-8c84-006d0dafc353" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9cdf5f93-9023-5100-b545-6c242d092410" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "c383c7f1-177e-5435-a49a-cf39f18edcfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "afd4c7e4-7e14-5574-80f0-31b37c14cae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75248/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-75248/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-75248/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-75248/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-75248/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-75248/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75248" :question/id #uuid "b715fe4d-32ff-5b40-a10a-915385f0e83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75248" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y-1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/b3c2a3891860a8ad88d2f111a3e279a65398a37020ab2f0843f8475dfd38df15.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/76cbbcf7ddff625cf035d6f7e37acbc6d278734cc87648ef6a88f8ace61f731e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=0 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =0+1+1+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-75248/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75248"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9476"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6242"] :question/problem "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=15 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=10 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6241"] :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0ad490af48e1db93ab9cae7e482b5eb8956aa14f8d2972816f8852179b35c4cb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a33927647d7651ce47c9e6619bd26a29ceb5cc5dd74e379bf81ea61806fb5c95.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=2+3+4+5=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
